urce-tracked points ledger build, from the accepted H40 blueprint. Korhan approved on 2026-11-27 decision 1 = A: H40-H42 (a source-tracked single points total) as the first ledger step on top of the current double-entry ledger, with H32 option A (daily partitions, no foreign keys to hot tables). The H40 blueprint is accepted and merged on main (PR 17, docs: docs/adr/0010-account-and-value-dictionary.md, 0010-migration-fixture.md, 0010-worked-movements.md, docs/security/threat-model.md; PR 18 .gitleaksignore). Ticket text and open records: run bin/fm-tasks-axi.sh show h41-h42-ledger-build --full in ~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred. Source of truth: ~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/hub-ewallet-altyapi/report.md (Turkish; sections 4, 7, 8 and 'H32 ve H35'), the PRD ~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/hub-prd/report.md, and ~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/h40-ledger-design/final-report.md. Security-critical money path: balances change only in the ledger service, every money movement is double-entry and idempotent.\012\012## Firstmate spec\012Build H41 first (expand migration, then backfill, read switch, write switch, contract as separate deliveries per the arcadista AGENTS.md migration rules), then H42, exactly as the ADR and ticket define them; the review of H41's expand migration approves Rulebook 1. Operator database logins are ticket H51, a precondition for H41's write switch: do not switch writes before it exists, and report that as a blocker if it is missing. Reversals never reopen or re-resolve hold allocations. OPEN H42 POINT, decide as a needs-decision before building it and never silently: reversal of a reversal after the original lot has expired (data/h40-ledger-design/nm-01M8E9PQ5WG8W5QAKTPRXCBEYQ-R5-note.md has options A restore-lot-from-new and B fresh operator grant; the author recommends A). Do not touch H11 or H32 (other workers own them); H32 will move to daily partitions separately. Merge bar (Korhan 2026-11-20): name the applicable rules of ~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/muhendislik-standardi/checklist.md in a 'Checklist rules in scope' PR-body section with evidence; an independent other-vendor audit checks each PR against them. Mac load rule: targeted local tests only, one run at a time through ~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/dotnet-lock.sh with -m:2 and MSBUILDDISABLENODEREUSE=1; export DOTNET_CLI_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT=1 TESTINGPLATFORM_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT=1 VSTEST_TELEMETRY_OPTEDIN=0 DOTNET_NOLOGO=1 in every dotnet command; full suite only in GitHub CI with the 'ci: merge' label on your PR. Test-first. Ask-user findings go to Firstmate; never answer one yourself. No em dash. Deliver H41 and H42 as separate PRs; report each PR when its checks are green.\012\012# Herdr lifecycle declaration - NOT ENABLED\012**HARD SAFETY GATE:** this scaffold cannot inspect the task text filled in above.\012If the task will start, stop, delete, restart, profile, or otherwise drive Herdr lifecycle behavior, stop and regenerate the brief with `--herdr-lab` before dispatch.\012Do not add Herdr lifecycle commands to this unguarded brief by hand.\012\012# Setup\012You are in a disposable git worktree of arcadista, at a detached HEAD on a clean default branch.\012\012**Verify isolation before anything else.** Run `pwd -P` and `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`; both must resolve to the disposable task worktree you were launched in, such as a treehouse pool path or an Orca-managed worktree, not the primary checkout firstmate operates from.\012The path check is authoritative: `git rev-parse --git-dir` and `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` can help inspect the repo, but they do not prove you are outside the primary checkout.\012If the top-level path is the primary checkout or not the worktree you were launched in, STOP - do not branch or commit here - append `blocked [at=<epoch>]: launched in primary checkout, not an isolated worktree` to the status file and stop.\012\0121. First action: create your branch: `git checkout -b fm/h41-h42-ledger-build --`\0122. Run `no-mistakes doctor`; if it reports the repo is not initialized here, run `no-mistakes init`.\012\012# Rules\0121. Never push to the default branch. Never merge a PR.\0122. Keep project edits inside this worktree; keep proof and scratch output outside it, under `~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/h41-h42-ledger-build/` or a temporary directory.\012   Outside the worktree, write only that task material and the status and steering-inbox records authorized below.\012   Leave the worktree clean before reporting done.\0123. Use gh-axi for GitHub operations and chrome-devtools-axi for browser operations.\0124. Report status by appending one line:\012   `echo "{state} [at=<epoch>]: {one short line}" >> '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/state/h41-h42-ledger-build.status' && { [ ! -e '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/config/fleet-ledger' ] || '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/bin/fm-fleet-ledger.sh' appended '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/config' '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/state/h41-h42-ledger-build.status' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; }`\012   States: working, needs-decision, blocked, paused, done, failed.\012   Substitute `<epoch>` with the current Unix time in seconds - run `date +%s` and write the number it printed; a stamp that is not plain digits records no time at all.\012   Each append wakes firstmate, so report sparingly: only phase changes a supervisor\012   would act on (setup done, bug reproduced, fix implemented, validation passed) and the\012   needs-decision/blocked/paused/done/failed states. No step-by-step FYI progress lines;\012   firstmate reads your pane for that.\012   Whenever you mention a PR anywhere - a status line, your terminal, a summary - write its full\012   https:// URL exactly as the forge printed it, never a bare number such as "PR 108"; firstmate\012   copies that URL from your line rather than assembling one.\012   A mid-task `working:` line (including setup complete) is nonterminal: do not end the\012   turn after it; continue the same stage until a defined `done:` gate under Definition of done.\012   Use `paused: {why}` - distinct from `blocked:` - when deliberately waiting for work or an external condition expected to clear on its own, including your own validation round.\012   Before ending your turn with your own background shell or monitor still running, or before waiting on your own pipeline run or a long foreground command, append `paused [at=<epoch>]: {job and completion condition}` to the status file.\012   Name what you are waiting for and what will let you resume; do not repeat the declaration on every poll.\012   Do not declare active implementation or reasoning as a wait.\012   Firstmate may still raise one first-sight alert; the declared wait then uses the existing long recheck cadence instead of repeated possible-wedge alarms.\012   When you know when the wait clears, include `until <YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MMZ>` (UTC) for a recheck at that time; for your own background job or command with a timeout, that time is its start plus its timeout, rounded up to the minute.\012   Follow the resolution rule below when the wait clears, then resume the task.\012   Use `blocked:` when you are stuck and need help.\012\0125. If you hit the same obstacle twice, append `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {why}` and stop; firstmate will help.\0126. If a decision belongs above the implementation worker (product choices, destructive actions),\012   append `needs-decision [at=<epoch>]: {summary of options}` and stop. Firstmate will reply with the decision.\012   For a no-mistakes ask-user gate specifically, escalate all ask-user findings as one event plus one snapshot file, using that same shape even when the gate holds only a single ask-user finding: write only the ask-user findings, verbatim and unparaphrased (id, severity, file, line, description, authority), to `~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/h41-h42-ledger-build/nm-<run>-findings.txt`, then report the gate with\012   `needs-decision [at=<epoch>] [key=nm-<run>-<step>]: ask-user findings=<id1>,<id2>,... file=~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/data/h41-h42-ledger-build/nm-<run>-findings.txt`\012   naming every ask-user finding id from that gate. The status line only points at the file; it never restates or summarizes a finding's content.\012   A decision or blocker you opened stays open until a `resolved` line carrying its exact key lands; a later `done:` or `working:` line never closes it, even when the answer is what started that work.\012   Firstmate's reply normally writes that closing line at answer time; when a blocker or wait clears WITHOUT a firstmate reply, append `resolved [at=<epoch>]: {how it cleared}` yourself (same `[key=<slug>]` if you opened it with one) as you resume.\0127. Never administer infrastructure that every lane shares. Two things are shared:\012   - The `no-mistakes` daemon - one instance serving every lane/home, so stopping, restarting, or\012     updating it kills other lanes' in-flight pipeline runs; only firstmate manages the daemon.\012     Before you append `blocked:` about the pipeline, run `no-mistakes daemon status` and\012     `no-mistakes axi status`. If the daemon socket refuses connections or is missing, append\012     `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {the daemon error}` and stop even when the local run record still says running or\012     fixing, because that record can be stale after the daemon exits. A run record failed with a\012     daemon error is also a real block.\012     Only after ruling out socket refusal, if the run is still running or fixing, reattach and keep\012     going. A drive-call error, timeout, slow read, or generic unreachability is NOT a daemon error:\012     the daemon accepts `respond` immediately and runs the round in the background, so a killed or\012     timed-out call was only waiting for a read while the run kept working.\012   - The worktree pool your own worktree came from, and the repository every lane's worktree\012     shares. Never create, remove, return, prune, move, or reassign a worktree or pool slot, and\012     never write into a sibling slot's directory. Rule 2 does not cover this: removing a worktree\012     is administration rather than an edit outside your directory, and it lands on lanes that are\012     running right now. The act is the rule and commands are only examples of it - `treehouse`\012     get/return/remove/prune, the equivalent operations on any other worktree provider or runtime\012     backend, and `git worktree add|remove|move|prune`. A slot that looks unused is not evidence\012     that it is free, and returning your own worktree is firstmate's job at cleanup, not yours.\012   If you genuinely need a second checkout, another slot, or the daemon touched, append\012   `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {what you need}` and stop; firstmate arranges it.\0128. Never start a shell command with `cd`, and never run a search whose path depends on the current directory.\012   Use absolute paths, `git -C <abs>`, or a `( cd <abs> && ... )` subshell.\012   Pass this rule to every sub-agent you launch.\012\012# Firstmate instruction inbox\012Firstmate steers you through durable message files in '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/state/h41-h42-ledger-build.inbox'.\012When a terminal message says an instruction is waiting there - and at any natural checkpoint when you are unsure - list them with `find '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/state/h41-h42-ledger-build.inbox' -maxdepth 1 -name '*.msg' 2>/dev/null | sort -V`, read the complete contents of each record (including its final line even when it is not newline-terminated) and act on each message in numeric order, then acknowledge each handled message by moving it: `mv '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/state/h41-h42-ledger-build.inbox'/NNN.msg '~/.treehouse/alfred-7bab20/10/alfred/state/h41-h42-ledger-build.inbox'/handled/`.\012Prefer the format-aware `fm_task_inb